  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Massif de la Sainte-Baume stretches along the border between the Var and the Bouches-du-Rhône. Listed as a regional nature park, it offers family-friendly trails suited to most levels, in a wooded setting quite different from the coast. The village of Plan-d'Aups-Sainte-Baume (686 m), with its Hostellerie de la Sainte-Baume, is the classic starting point towards the Grotte de Sainte-Marie-Madeleine, a pilgrimage site tucked into the cliff. The massif is also crossed by two long-distance hiking routes, the GR9 and the GR98.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the Sainte-Baume regional nature park, dogs must be kept on a leash in the Sainte-Baume biological reserve and are banned at the grotto. Livestock guardian dogs may also be present on some routes: go around the flock and keep your dog close. From June to September, access to the forest massifs is regulated by the Var and Bouches-du-Rhône prefectures because of fire risk (maps published every evening at 6pm): check them before setting off.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: take the chance to stop at Plan-d'Aups-Sainte-Baume, a good stop before or after the walk — and check the day's massif access map in summer before setting out on the trails.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The village of Plan-d'Aups-Sainte-Baume, at the foot of the massif, is handy for a break before getting back on the road.</p>
